WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.967 --> 00:00:09.081
Marcus Bell: Hey Ashley, how's it going?

2
00:00:09.846 --> 00:00:12.863
Ashley Moore: Hey Marcus, doing okay. Busy week. How about you?

3
00:00:13.864 --> 00:00:22.041
Marcus Bell: Good, good. Just working through end-of-quarter stuff. Wanted to check in, see how things are going with call tracking and Signal AI.

4
00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:30.602
Ashley Moore: Call tracking's been fine. We're seeing maybe 1,800 calls a month across the six locations, which is up a bit from summer.

5
00:00:31.104 --> 00:00:35.684
Marcus Bell: Nice, that's a decent bump. Anything driving that, or just season?

6
00:00:36.908 --> 00:00:42.040
Ashley Moore: Probably season plus the Google Ads push we did in September. Termite stuff mostly.

7
00:00:42.624 --> 00:00:45.840
Marcus Bell: Makes sense. How's the team handling the volume?

8
00:00:46.487 --> 00:00:55.810
Ashley Moore: We're a little thin. One of our Raleigh dispatchers is out on leave until November, so everyone's covering. It's been a juggle.

9
00:00:56.447 --> 00:01:02.439
Marcus Bell: Got it. Let me know if you need anything from our side, even just reporting help.

10
00:01:03.685 --> 00:01:08.234
Ashley Moore: Will do. Actually, there is something I've been meaning to bring up.

11
00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:09.723
Marcus Bell: Sure, go ahead.

12
00:01:10.096 --> 00:01:16.826
Ashley Moore: So the spam detection in Signal AI has been flagging calls that aren't spam. Specifically after-hours and weekend calls.

13
00:01:18.194 --> 00:01:20.608
Marcus Bell: Okay. How often are we talking?

14
00:01:21.799 --> 00:01:36.072
Ashley Moore: I pulled a couple weeks of data. About 30 calls in September got marked spam, and when I listened to them, most were real customers calling after hours about ants or a wasp nest or whatever.

15
00:01:36.072 --> 00:01:38.847
Ashley Moore: Those are gone from our reporting now.

16
00:01:39.913 --> 00:01:44.209
Marcus Bell: So they're being excluded from the numbers you look at.

17
00:01:44.863 --> 00:01:52.113
Ashley Moore: Right. And that's a problem because we run a weekend crew, and those calls are a big chunk of weekend bookings.

18
00:01:52.113 --> 00:02:00.743
Ashley Moore: If they're tagged as spam, it looks like nobody's calling, and I can't tell the owners we had a slow weekend when we actually didn't.

19
00:02:02.026 --> 00:02:05.426
Marcus Bell: How are you catching it? Just spot-checking, or something else?

20
00:02:05.952 --> 00:02:15.878
Ashley Moore: I noticed it because our call volume for Saturdays looked weirdly low two weeks in a row. So I went into the transcript list and started listening.

21
00:02:15.878 --> 00:02:17.716
Ashley Moore: That's when I found them.

22
00:02:18.521 --> 00:02:22.931
Marcus Bell: Okay. Is it happening on specific numbers, or across all six locations?

23
00:02:23.804 --> 00:02:30.805
Ashley Moore: I've seen it on the Raleigh and Durham numbers mostly, but I haven't checked the others closely.

24
00:02:31.579 --> 00:02:37.180
Marcus Bell: And is it just the after-hours piece, or are daytime calls getting hit too?

25
00:02:37.929 --> 00:02:43.542
Ashley Moore: Daytime looks fine. It's the late-night ones, like after 9pm, and some Sunday mornings.

26
00:02:44.951 --> 00:02:54.779
Marcus Bell: Got it. So if a call comes in at 11pm and gets flagged, it drops out of the spam-excluded view and you lose it.

27
00:02:55.261 --> 00:03:06.869
Ashley Moore: Exactly. And I can see it in the transcript list, but the reporting doesn't count it. So I'm manually re-checking, which is not how I want to spend my Monday.

28
00:03:08.119 --> 00:03:14.022
Marcus Bell: Yeah, that's not sustainable. Can you send me a few examples? Call IDs or timestamps, anything you have.

29
00:03:14.522 --> 00:03:19.098
Ashley Moore: Yeah, I can put together a list. I've got about eight I saved.

30
00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:30.728
Marcus Bell: Perfect. I'll take that to the product team and see what they say. I can't promise a fix today, but I'll make sure it's in front of the right people.

31
00:03:32.207 --> 00:03:36.557
Ashley Moore: That's all I'm asking. Just want to know it's being looked at.

32
00:03:37.566 --> 00:03:39.669
Marcus Bell: Understood. Anything else on your list?

33
00:03:40.547 --> 00:03:44.221
Ashley Moore: No, that's the main thing. Everything else is fine.

34
00:03:44.742 --> 00:03:47.225
Marcus Bell: Okay. How's the Google Ads connection doing?

35
00:03:47.808 --> 00:03:49.182
Ashley Moore: Fine. No issues there.

36
00:03:49.521 --> 00:03:54.284
Marcus Bell: Good. And how's the new hire working out? The one you mentioned last time.

37
00:03:55.070 --> 00:04:04.147
Ashley Moore: Oh, Dana? She's great. Picking things up fast. She's actually the one who flagged the weekend volume drop to me first.

38
00:04:04.737 --> 00:04:12.332
Marcus Bell: Nice, glad to hear it. Alright, send me those call examples when you get a chance, and I'll follow up by Wednesday.

39
00:04:12.635 --> 00:04:14.188
Ashley Moore: Will do. Thanks, Marcus.

40
00:04:14.715 --> 00:04:16.233
Marcus Bell: Thanks, Ashley. Talk soon.

41
00:04:17.001 --> 00:04:17.801
Ashley Moore: Bye.
